CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md

# Gregor's working log. Kept locally, deliberately not published: it is a
# candid record of what was tried and what broke, including other people's
# defects. An ignore rule alone would not have untracked it — it was removed
# from the index with `git rm --cached`, and this line stops `git add .`
# putting it back. Note the file stays in history before that commit.
GREGOR_WORKLOG.md

# Internal working documents, untracked before the jury hand-in. Same mechanism
# as the line above: `git rm --cached` did the untracking, these lines stop
# `git add .` putting them back, and every file stays on disk and in history.
#
# CLAUDE-CODE-PROMPT.md          how to point an AI at this repo — invites the
#                                wrong conversation with a reviewer
# PITCH-PLAN.md                  the stage script next to the list of gaps
# PROJECT_COMPLETE_OVERVIEW.md   superseded by README.md + TECHNICAL_DETAILS.md
# docs/TASK-*.md                 work assignments for named people
#
# Kept on purpose, do NOT add here: PLAYBOOK.md (43 citations from code,
# judge.py and scoring.py:251 among them), PROJECT-STATE.md (technical debt
# #12/#14/#15 are cited by number in comments), SECRETS.md (placeholders only,
# it documents the discipline), tools/art50check.py (736df3d: origin of the
# 0-of-24 finding).
CLAUDE-CODE-PROMPT.md
PITCH-PLAN.md
PROJECT_COMPLETE_OVERVIEW.md
docs/TASK-GREGOR.md
docs/TASK-KWABENA.md
docs/TASK-VLAD.md

# Never commit secrets
#
# `.env` and `.env.*` carry no slash, so they already match at any depth -
# lab/.env, deploy/.env and calibration/.env are covered by those two lines.
# What they do NOT cover is a secret file whose name merely ends in .env, and
# lab/.env.example names two of those as things people are likely to create.
# So: any *.env, plus the one named file it warns about.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.env
lab/bots/keys.yaml
*.key
*.pem
credentials.json
secrets.yaml

# Local Compose overrides. Machine-specific by definition (a port remap, a
# different volume), and untracked is not the same as ignored: `git add -A`
# sweeps up whatever is lying around, and this repository is public.
docker-compose.override.yml

